at are being inordinately impacted as  well so the need is great and this has  to be an all-in fight so i'm so glad  you're having me to talk about it  well you know i think a lot of americans  like myself would be astounded  to know that this is happening in the  united states in 2020  the richest most powerful country in the  world you mentioned some of the people  who would most  be impacted by this describe others  besides the children of course who are  going to be severely impacted by  all this yeah if you'll indulge me i'll  talk about the kids for one more moment  um so first ordinarily without a  pandemic there are about 22 million kids  around the country who rely on free and  reduced lunches  for many of those children the only  consistent access to  a nutritious meal that they get is at  school so while we  try to figure out how to get kids back  to school safely  there are children out there who are  hungry right now and who need help  two other communities that are  inordinately impacted are communities of  color  not only are communities of color about  two times more likely to be  food insecure they're also two times  more likely to suffer from some of the  key  chronic illnesses that make covet 19  more lethal  so they're dealing with so many issues  right now making an already vulnerable  community  even more vulnerable and then rural  america this is an area that is not  often talked about  that i want to make certain that that  your audience is aware of as well  of the communities around the country  over 80 percent of the most  food insecure counties in this country  are in rural america  so the need is great it's deep it cuts  across  all types of demographics across this  country there's a lot of need and i  think that we're well positioned to help  people right now when they need them  when they need us the most and i hope  that we will  claire i recently had an opportunity to  speak to a grassroots organization  that is stocking sidewalk refrigerators  with free food and they're also helping  the community to become self-sustainable  what programs are  you working with to ensure that because  it's one thing to provide those meals  it's another  for these communities to be able to  actually feed themselves  you're absolutely right i will not nor  will my network be satisfied  to feed an increasingly growing line we  must do some things to address some of  the systemic needs that give rise to the  line  we want the line to shrink we want to do  a great job  with the people who come to us for help  we don't need them to help us  to need us in the way that they do now  so a lot of the work that we do  is around interventions we refer to it  as our empower work  it's work programmatic design that we do  going into communities  and helping communities to have  microphones to speak for themselves  asking them what do they need meeting  them where they are and helping them to  move forward  that's a lot of the work that we do at  feeding america  during this covet crisis one of the  benefits i believe of of investing in  the work of feeding america  is that we have a national lens that we  can apply and we ask our food banks and  our agencies around the country  to assess and give us estimates of where  the most acute needs are  and then through the gifting that we're  able to provide or grants that we're  able to provide through  through donations that we receive we're  able to go right where the need  is so we've made inordinate investments  in each of these three communities that  are so fragile right now  so that's uh hopefully we can continue  to do that work with additional  investments  from you see behind me  feedingamerica.org  that's a great way for your audience to  help with this effort  by going through feedingamerica.org and  making any type of donation donation  that they can  we will then reinvest them in the  community they can also use that website  to find a food bank  near them food banks are spread out  across this country  and they're there inside of those  communities helping people  on the ground i've had the great  privilege during this pandemic of going  out  and seeing the need and witnessing i've  been out there on the front lines with  many of our members  and they're really stepping up to this  challenge i think they should be  commended but as i said they definitely  need help so right now  we're encouraging congress and we ask  your audience to also encourage congress  to get back to that negotiating table  and to look at some federal  interventions that can do some things  that we can't  yes well the work that you are doing uh  claire is so very important so very  vital  uh to the citizens of this country and  we thank you for it and as you say  people can go to feedingamerica.org for  more information uh claire thank you so  much we appreciate it
 

